WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:09.522
Hi! The amount of space a solid takes up is its volume. Today we'll measure volume with unit cubes.

2
00:00:09.672 --> 00:00:13.266
This is a cube with edges of 1 centimetre.

3
00:00:13.416 --> 00:00:21.306
Its volume is 1 cubic centimetre. We write it as 1 cm with a small 3.

4
00:00:21.456 --> 00:00:29.202
Let's build a cuboid with unit cubes. Look at the bottom layer first: 3 by 2 makes 6 cubes.

5
00:00:29.352 --> 00:00:33.642
There are 2 layers, so 6 times 2 is 12 cubes.

6
00:00:33.792 --> 00:00:38.730
So the volume is 12 cubic centimetres.

7
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.738
We can draw solids on isometric dot paper.

8
00:00:42.888 --> 00:00:48.762
Upright edges are drawn straight up and down. The other edges slant along the dots.

9
00:00:48.912 --> 00:00:58.578
This staircase shape has 3 plus 2 plus 1, which is 6 unit cubes. Its volume is 6 cubic centimetres.

10
00:00:58.728 --> 00:01:02.850
One more: how many unit cubes make this big cube?

11
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.322
We can see 7 of them.

12
00:01:05.472 --> 00:01:13.794
But there is 1 hidden at the back, underneath! Each layer has 4, and there are 2 layers, so there are 8.

13
00:01:13.944 --> 00:01:19.986
Watch out! Some pupils count only the cubes they can see. That is wrong!

14
00:01:20.136 --> 00:01:28.674
The hidden cubes at the back and underneath count too. Count layer by layer: 4 in each layer, 2 layers, so 8.

15
00:01:28.824 --> 00:01:33.642
Counting layer by layer means you won't miss any.

16
00:01:33.792 --> 00:01:46.842
Your turn! The bottom layer is 4 by 2 unit cubes, and there are 3 layers. What is the volume in cubic centimetres? Pause and think.

17
00:01:46.992 --> 00:01:56.970
Each layer has 8, and 3 layers make 24. The volume is 24 cubic centimetres. Did you get it right?

18
00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:09.282
Another one! A cuboid is built from 1 centimetre cubes, 3 by 3 by 2. What is its volume? Pause and think.

19
00:02:09.432 --> 00:02:19.722
Each layer has 9 cubes, and 2 layers make 18. The volume is 18 cubic centimetres. Did you get it right?

20
00:02:19.872 --> 00:02:31.554
Another one! A row has 5 small cubes, each 1 cubic centimetre. What is the volume? Pause and think.

21
00:02:31.704 --> 00:02:39.498
5 cubes of 1 cubic centimetre make 5 cubic centimetres. Did you get it right?

22
00:02:39.648 --> 00:02:52.722
Last one! Each layer has 2 rows of 5 cubes, and there are 2 layers. How many 1 cubic centimetre cubes is that? Pause and think.

23
00:02:52.872 --> 00:03:04.290
Each layer has 2 times 5, which is 10 cubes. 2 layers make 20 cubes, so the volume is 20 cubic centimetres. Did you get it right?

24
00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:13.434
Remember: volume is the number of unit cubes, measured in cubic centimetres. Count layer by layer, and include the hidden ones.

25
00:03:13.584 --> 00:03:17.778
Now go and practise in your garden!

